   and 5.0 A, the worst can scaled in proportion to the front end's current; the dense script drafts/scripts/ripple_dense.py is
   not in this tree, so the method is VIN-blind and is applied at the current the fault can hold continuously)
   R11 8 mOhm, 7.262 A: worst can 2.69 A matched, 2.70 A at 1.5:1, 3.11 A at 2:1 against 2.8 A: matched met, 1.5:1 met, 2:1 NOT MET
     the bank's worst can per ampere must be at most 1.042 / 1.036 / 0.901 times today's (matched / 1.5:1 / 2:1); at 9 V, where the peak
     limit holds the output to 4.57 A, it reads 1.69 / 1.70 / 1.95 A
   R11 7 mOhm, 8.300 A: worst can 3.07 A matched, 3.09 A at 1.5:1, 3.55 A at 2:1 against 2.8 A: matched NOT MET, 1.5:1 NOT MET, 2:1 NOT MET
     the bank's worst can per ampere must be at most 0.912 / 0.907 / 0.788 times today's (matched / 1.5:1 / 2:1); at 9 V, where the peak
     limit holds the output to 4.57 A, it reads 1.69 / 1.70 / 1.95 A
   DECISION for B-4: the bank is rated, not bounded (the average limit holds the highest permitted current above the boundary
   VIN at both outcomes, and no peak or valley limit compatible with service holds it lower). Closure OWED to the generator owner:
   the dense node analysis re-run with a re-sized bank (more EEHZK1V331P, one part number, or ceramics moved to FE_OUT) at the
   chosen current; INCONCLUSIVE until then; bench 7b.8

7. THE CONSEQUENCE FOR L4-E4
   B-1 and B-2 close at both outcomes by the same R12 (the peak limit does not depend on R11); B-4 does not close on the drawn
   bank at either, and its gap is 0.31 A at 2:1 only at 8 mOhm against 0.75 A at all three spreads at 7 mOhm. This decision
   SUPPORTS R11 8 mOhm (C2904240) with IIN_HOST 4.70 A and L4-E5's line; it does not require 7 mOhm. For 8 mOhm to stand, bench
   V-A07 must show the pin's error at 10 mOhm at most 0.071 A between 26.50 and 27.24 V (L4-E5's figure, unchanged here: R12 is not
   in the ISNS path). If it fails, 7 mOhm stands with the same R12 and a larger bank re-size

8. ACCEPTANCE PREDICATES (held by v2/ecad/tools/tests/test_l4e6.py on these computed values)
   0: the three outputs reproduced byte for byte in child processes: yes
   0: l4e4_limits.compute() and r11_dep.main() print their records in-process; the FET relations reprint r11_dep.out's: yes
   (a) hiccup counts cycle-by-cycle limits only; with R12 as drawn it is not guaranteed at 9 V at either outcome: yes
   (a) alone leaves B-1 over 90 % of the typical Isat at 9 V at both outcomes: yes
   (a) alone leaves a FET past 150 C at 9 V at both outcomes on the maker's RthetaJA: yes
   the chosen R12 is the smallest catalogue value that closes B-1 (25 C) and B-2 at both outcomes with service margin: yes
   B-1 at 25 C: L1's peak at most 90 % of the typical Isat at both outcomes, 9 to 36 V: yes
   B-1 at temperature: INCONCLUSIVE (the held Coilcraft sheet carries no temperature derating): yes
   B-2: every FET at most 150 C at 9, 15.1 and 36 V at both outcomes (RthetaJA the maker's, an ASSUMPTION for board A): yes
   B-4 on the drawn bank: NOT met at the 2:1 spread at either outcome; met matched and at 1.5:1 at 8 mOhm only: yes
   service: the chosen peak limit's minimum above H3's in-service boost peak; the valley limit's minimum above the pin path's valley: yes
   the chosen R12 needs L4-E5's line first: L4-E4's 4.964 A alone would meet the peak limit below 16.3 V: yes
   the consequence for L4-E4: 8 mOhm supported (B-1, B-2 independent of R11; B-4's gap smaller at 8 mOhm): yes

END. Desk arithmetic; nothing is measured. B-1 at temperature, the comparator's delay, the loop at the new R12 and the
re-sized bank stay INCONCLUSIVE until the documents, the generator owner's analyses and the bench rows of L4E6-FAULT-HANDLING.md.
